# Block structure algorithm

Status: draft for a future replacement of the block structure clauses. The existing clauses remain normative until this draft is approved. This section specifies ownership and extent; the lexical productions specify valid marker spellings, and inline and rendering rules specify content within the resulting blocks.

Read normalized input as lines. Expand tabs to four-column stops for structural comparisons, but keep the authored characters in content. A parser holds a document frame, a path of open container frames, and at most one open leaf at the end of that path. Each frame stores its authored base, marker column, content column if any, closer if any, last boundary, and current paragraph claim. A claim belongs only to the deepest open paragraph. An invisible line ends that claim without ending its owner. A closed child leaves its owner open but leaves no paragraph claim.

For each line, perform these steps in order:

1. Test an active code, raw, or comment payload against its own closer before reading any other marker. Otherwise take the open frames from outside inward. Strip a quote marker or the indentation up to a body content column when the frame's continuation cell allows it. A blank line reaches an indentation body but misses an unmarked quote. Stop at the first miss. Test an exact colon closer at the frame's authored base before opening a block in that frame.
2. Classify a comment before visible ownership. In the remaining line, choose the deepest frame reached in step 1. Below a body content column, choose the nearest reached ancestor. A quote is reached by its marker, not by indentation. Recognize the line in that owner's coordinates: blank, invisible definition or attribute, valid opener, closer, caption, attachment, or ordinary text. An opener at a body content column belongs to that body; a sibling item marker belongs to the enclosing list. A line may open a block past a marker body's minimum column at its own authored base. At document, quote, or colon-body level, an opener other than a list marker starts at that frame's exact content column.
3. If the line missed an open descendant, keep the deepest paragraph only when its row permits lazy continuation, the line is nonblank, and its recognized form does not interrupt a paragraph. Follow the last child at every depth; do not revive an earlier paragraph. A list marker does not interrupt an open paragraph, except a sibling marker in an already open list. Headings, thematic breaks, quotes, terms, tables, valid colon openers, guarded code openers, and invisible lines do interrupt. A code opener interrupts only if its matching closer is reachable in its host. A caption attaches instead of folding only when its table row allows it. An invalid opener is text. A comment seen below a body column retains that body's ownership for the next noninterrupting below-column line only when the body had a paragraph claim or retained ownership before the comment. A blank or comment at the body column clears this retention; a sibling marker or interrupting opener is classified first.
4. If step 3 did not fold the line, close every missed frame, then apply its recognized form in the surviving owner. A blank or invisible line ends a paragraph. A valid container opener adds a frame and classifies any marker-line remainder in that new frame. A leaf opener ends the preceding leaf and adds a leaf. Ordinary text starts or extends a paragraph. A closed block leaves no paragraph claim. Record blank runs for list grouping and tightness. End of input closes every frame.

The table supplies every container policy. "Base" means the authored column after enclosing prefixes have been removed. "Reach" means the line supplies the stated prefix or content column. A blank line has no paragraph claim. One attached block can span lines, but its grant ends at that block's boundary. An accepted `+` interrupts an open paragraph. A refused `+` is text in its ordinary owner; it folds only into a paragraph that remains open. A later `+` cannot reopen the paragraph the first marker ended.

|= Family |= Start condition |= Continuation condition |= Lazy allowed |= Blank-line behavior |= End condition |= Rendered element |
| List item | Valid bullet, task, decimal, alphabetic, roman, or bare-dot marker with content. Content column is after marker and separator, ignoring marker attributes. Same-column items group only when marker kind, delimiter, dialect, and task state agree. | Nonblank line reaches content column; a marker at that column opens a child list. A sibling marker opens the next item. A quote opened on the item's marker line with an opaque fence keeps over-indented followers as item text until a blank or quoted paragraph ends that fence tail; a line reaching the item's content column still opens there. | Yes, while the deepest paragraph remains open and the interruption test permits it. | A later line reaching the body keeps the item. Blank between items or before a second paragraph makes the item loose; blank before a child block alone does not. Three blanks before a sibling start a new list. | Sibling marker, missed column without lazy claim, enclosing boundary, or input end. | `<li>` in `<ul>` or `<ol>`; task items carry a checkbox. |
| Definition body | Valid `: ` while a term in the same owner remains open; its separator width sets the content column. | Nonblank line reaches that column. The preceding term has no body column: past its owner's content column, non-list visible lines fold into it, definitions stay text, and comments stay invisible without ending it. At that column, an opener or invisible line ends the term. | Yes, by the shared paragraph test. | A later reached line keeps the body; paragraph separation makes the definition list loose. | Next term or body, missed column without claim, host boundary, or input end. | `<dd>` in `<dl>`. |
| Footnote body | Valid `[^label]: `; content column is two past its marker column. | Nonblank line reaches that column. | Yes, by the shared paragraph test. | A later reached line keeps the body. | Missed column without claim, host boundary, or input end. | Collected definition; referenced body renders as an endnote. |
| Quote | `>` followed by one space or line end; each prefix opens one level. | Repeated quote prefix at that level. | Yes, from the deepest open paragraph, across missed prefixes at any depth. An unmarked list marker folds at any column. An unmarked block opener folds except at the enclosing host's content column; document-level indented openers remain text. | An unmarked blank ends the quote; a marked blank keeps it and ends its paragraph. | Missed prefix without lazy claim, host boundary, or input end. | `<blockquote>`; a caption may wrap it as a figure. |
| Colon fence family | Valid run of at least three colons. Bare, typed, quoted, `figure`, `>`, `|`, and backslash forms select the body and output mode. A bare `figure` inside another figure group is a typed div. | Every line inside the host reaches the body until its exact closer; `|` mode keeps lines as payload, and other modes read blocks. | Block mode uses the child paragraph test; payload mode has no block paragraph. | Blank is body content or block separation in its mode. | Exact-length colon run at authored base, host boundary, or input end. | `<div>`, `<aside>`, figure group, quote, line block, hard-break block, or placed directive according to opener. |
| Code, raw, comment fence | Valid code or raw opener; matched comment opener. Code opener inside a paragraph requires a reachable closer. | Payload is opaque; no block marker is read within it. | No. | Payload keeps its lines; it creates no paragraph claim. | Matching code run at its base, exact comment run, host boundary, or input end as the fence kind requires. | `<pre><code>`, matching raw output, or invisible comment. |
| Line block | `::: |`; shares the colon frame. | Lines are payload, preserving line breaks and leading space. | No. | Blank is a payload line. | Exact colon closer or host boundary. | Line block with preserved breaks. |
| Table | Valid pipe row or selected CSV or TSV fence. A continuation row needs an earlier body row, beyond the header and delimiter. | Following valid row or row continuation. | No. | Blank ends the row run. | First nonrow, host boundary, or input end. | `<table>`; an eligible caption may attach. |
| Caption slot | `^ ` with content after an eligible image paragraph, quote, table, code block, display-math paragraph, figure-group closer, or fenced-quote closer. | Plain inline line, while the caption paragraph remains open. | Yes, by the paragraph interruption test; another `^ ` ends it. | One blank between host and marker is allowed; two detach. A blank after its start ends the caption. | Interrupting line, second caption marker, host boundary, or input end. | `<figcaption>` or table `<caption>` on its host. |
| `+` attachment | Lone `+` at a live quote or marker body's marker column, or as its first-block content. | A separate-line marker grants the next block at its host marker column. A first-block list item takes its next owned block, including one at its content column; a block at an enclosing item's column stays there. Other first-block hosts accept a non-list block at document column zero or their own marker column; an intervening column refuses the grant. A sibling list item is ineligible. | No claim of its own. The attached block may leave a claim. | A blank or missing next block refuses the grant. | End of the one attached block. | No element; changes the attached block's owner. |

All rows use the same ownership steps. Terms, headings, thematic breaks, paragraphs, invisible definitions, and attribute lines are leaves or tokens, not additional container policies. A list marker can end a term and open a list. A structural-only line changes caption scope only when it opens or closes a container; an empty quote has no caption target. A collected definition has no visible placeholder. A pending attribute belongs to its owner and expires when that owner closes. Rendering details inside the elements named above remain in their semantic clauses.
